    "description": "Maple CMS is a small public website with separate URLs for the home page, article pages, tag search, and a newsletter form. HomePageController, ArticlePageController, TagSearchController, and NewsletterController currently share one dispatching servlet, but each has different inputs, validation rules, repository calls, and response pages. ArticlePageController must read an article slug, reject malformed values, load the article and comments, and select either an article page or a not-found page. TagSearchController must combine a query, tag, and pagination cursor, while NewsletterController must validate an email address, execute a subscription workflow, and choose between confirmation and error output. The team wants each logical page or form to have a clear home for request-specific behavior rather than placing that behavior in HTML files or extending the shared servlet with branches for every screen. The public site has little common request processing beyond ordinary logging, so introducing a large infrastructure layer solely for routing would be undesirable. Domain services may be shared, but transport parsing and view selection must remain independently testable for each page.",
    "decision_statement": "Should each page or form have a dedicated component that owns input parsing, validation, domain invocation, and response selection, or should one shared component multiplex all of these behaviors?",
    "notes": "page-controller fits the per-page ownership of parsing, validation, and view selection with little shared processing; front-controller is the central-multiplexer alternative, with action-domain-responder and template-view supporting the action and response split."
  },
